1. e4 { Notes by Simon Alapin } 1... e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Bb4 5. O-O O-O 6. d3 d6 7. Bg5 Bxc3 8. bxc3 Ne7 { This is the usual
move introduced by Pillsbury, the alternative 8...Qe7 is not
very attractive. } 9. Bc4 { The usual Bxf6 is better, but
Janowski had a predilection for preserving his 2 Bishops. } 9... Ng6 10. Nh4 { ? } 10... Nf4 { ! This very strong move was first made by
Pillsbury. } 11. Bxf4 exf4 12. Nf3 { Loss of time; 12 g3 followed
by Ng2 was preferable. } 12... Bg4 13. h3 Bh5 14. Rb1 b6 15. Qd2 { Unnecessary, but Janowski evidently hoped for attacking
chances on the open g-file. } 15... Bxf3 16. gxf3 Nh5 17. Kh2 Qf6 18. Rg1 Rae8 19. d4 Kh8 20. Rb5 Qh6 21. Rbg5 f6 22. R5g4 g6 23. Bd3 { So as to stop ...f5. } 23... Re7 24. c4 { A careless move: White might
have tried something more passive such as Qc1 or Rg2, but he
was under great time pressure. } 24... Ng7 { ! } 25. c3 { ? d5! } 25... Ne6 26. Bf1 f5 { ! } 27. R4g2 Rf6 { ! } 28. Bd3 g5 { ! } 29. Rh1 { Only now
White sees the terrible threat of the Queen sacrifice, but
there is no defence anyway. } 29... g4 30. Be2 Ng5 31. fxg4 f3 32. Rg3 fxe2 0-1

