1. e4 { Notes by Emanuel Lasker } 1... e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 Na5 9. Bc2 c5 10. d4 Qc7 11. Nbd2 Nc6 12. h3 O-O 13. Nf1 cxd4 14. cxd4 Nxd4 15. Nxd4 exd4 { So far it's exactly like the third game of the match when I
continued 16 Ng3. } 16. Bg5 { The idea being either to get rid of
Black's important King's Bishop or to hamper its
development. The position is now getting very complicated. } 16... h6 17. Bh4 Qb6 { This is much too passive a move and Black will
almost immediately get into trouble. } 18. Qd3 { The threat being
19 Bxf6, followed by e5. Black can't parry this by 18...Be6 on
account of 19 Bxf6 Bc4 20 Qxd4, hence the loosening of Black's
K-wing is practically forced. } 18... g5 19. Bg3 Be6 20. Rad1 { ! } 20... Rfc8 21. Bb1 Nd7 22. e5 Nf8 23. Qf3 { Threatening exd6 followed by
Qf6. } 23... d5 24. Qh5 Kg7 25. f4 { Threatening f5. } 25... f5 { Like an
ancient Roman hero Tarrasch throws himself on his own sword!
Evidently he did not feel like offering some prolonged
resistance by 25...Ng6. } 26. exf6+ Bxf6 27. fxg5 hxg5 28. Be5 d3+ 29. Kh1 Ng6 30. Qxg5 Bf7 31. Ng3 Bxe5 32. Rxe5 Rh8 { ...Qd8 would
have been countered by 33 Re7. } 33. Bxd3 Ra7 34. Rde1 Kf8 35. Bxg6 Qxg6 36. Qe3 Rc7 37. Nf5 Qc6 38. Qg5 1-0

