1. d4 { Notes by Fred Reinfeld } 1... d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bf4 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Rc1 c5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. cxd5 exd5 { Not
...Nxd5?, or account of 9 Nxd5, with an attack on the
Bishop. After the text-move, though, 9 Nxd5 would be
ineffective. } 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. Bd3 d4 11. exd4 Nxd4 12. O-O { It
would be safer to exchange on d4, prior to castling. } 12... Bg4 13. Nb5 { ? This 'attack' is uncalled for; after 13 Be2 White
would have had a tenable game. } 13... Bxf3 14. gxf3 Ne6 { ! } 15. Be5 Nh5 16. Kh1 Qg5 17. Bg3 Rad8 { ! Typical for Lasker: a
positional manoeuvre combined with a tactical threat. } 18. Qc2 Qh6 { The point of Lasker's trap is this that the natural move
19 Rfd1 would now be faulty, in as much as ...Rc8 would give
Black a win in every variation. } 19. Rcd1 Rc8 { ! Having
interfered with the opponent's natural development Lasker now
regroups his forces. For much the same manoeuvre see his games
against Janowski (Petersburg 1914) and Rubinstein (Moscow
1925). } 20. Qb3 a6 21. Nc3 Nd4 { ! A P-sacrifice which should
secure at least a draw. } 22. Qxb7 Nxg3+ 23. fxg3 Rb8 24. Qxa6 Rb6 25. Qc4 Rxb2 26. h4 { ? By 26 Ne2 White could have forced a
draw, but Steinitz evidently means to play for a win. Indeed,
his position looks fairly secure. } 26... Ba7 27. Be4 Qd6 { ! Stopping
Ne2 for good and all and all practically forcing the next
move. } 28. f4 Qd7 29. Bg2 Qg4 { ! Very pretty since 30 Rxd4 can
now be countered by ...Qxg3. } 30. Qd3 Nf5 31. Ne4 Be3 { Much
better than ...Nxh4 and putting paid to Steinitz's excellent
defence. } 32. Rf3 Rxg2 { ! } 33. Kxg2 Nxh4+ 34. Kh2 Nxf3+ 35. Kg2 Nh4+ 36. Kh2 Nf5 37. Rb1 h5 38. Rb5 Ra8 39. a3 Rxa3 { If 40 Qxa3 Black mates in 4; nor could White save himself by
Qd8+. } 0-1

