1. d4 { Notes by R. Teichmann } 1... d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. e3 b6 7. Rc1 Bb7 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 c5 10. O-O Nbd7 11. dxc5 Nxc5 { Instead of this move, which leaves the d-pawn
isolated, I prefer ...bxc5. The two pawns can in this
variation be sufficiently supported, and will form the basis
of a counter-attack on the Queen's side. } 12. Bb1 Rc8 13. Bf5 Ne6 14. Bh4 Kh8 { This is certainly a weakening move, but the
position is very delicate and Black is at a loss what to do. } 15. Ne5 Qe8 16. Qf3 { Threatening Qh3 followed by Nxf7. } 16... Ng8 17. Bg3 Nh6 18. Bb1 f6 19. Nd3 Qd7 20. Qd1 Rfd8 21. Nf4 Nxf4 { This
exchange is ill-judged and causes serious trouble. It would
have been better to retire the knight to f8, where it would
support h7, and then to try a counter-attack on the Queen's
side, where his Rooks are in commanding positions. } 22. Bxf4 g5 { And this premature advance compromises his King's side
irretrievably. An attack on the King's side is, on the face of
it, without prospect. } 23. Bg3 Bf8 24. Qd3 f5 { Creating another
weakness. One of these two weak Pawns (d5 and f5) must fall in
a few moves. } 25. Ne2 Bg7 26. Nd4 Rf8 27. Rxc8 Qxc8 28. Qd2 { Very
subtle. The Queen is excellently posted here, and the Rook can
now take possession of the important c-file. } 28... Rf7 29. Rc1 Qe8 30. h4 gxh4 31. Bxh4 Bc8 32. Bg3 Ng4 33. Nb5 { This powerful move
leaves Black defenseless. The whole game was very well played
by Mr. Lasker, and it is a good specimen of this variation of
the Queen's Gambit, in which White maintains an advantage
throughout by combining a direct attack on the King with a
continuous pressure on the weak isolated d-pawn. } 33... Rf8 34. Nd6 Qe6 35. Rxc8 Rxc8 36. Bxf5 { and Black resigned. } 1-0

